WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:21.820
- Hi everyone, welcome to the
daily Homeroom livestream.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.880
Very excited about the
conversation we're about to have.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.060
I will start with my standard announcement

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.253
to remind everyone that we are

00:00:28.253 --> 00:00:31.110
a not-for-profit organization,
and we can only exist

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.610
with support from donors like yourself.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:35.280
I do wanna give a special shout-out

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.350
to several corporations and partners

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:38.680
that have stepped up
in the last few weeks,

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:39.600
especially when they realized

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.040
we were running in a
deficit even before COVID,

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.260
and the deficit got
larger as we tried to have

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.410
higher server capacity and more programs.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.040
So special thanks to Bank of America,

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.550
Google.org, AT&amp;T, Fastly, and Novartis,

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.130
and just a reminder, we
continue to run at a deficit.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:55.710
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.000
please think about making
a donation to Khan Academy

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.440
so that we can serve many, many,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.420
tens of millions of more
for a long time to come.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.370
With that, I'm really excited.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:10.370
Our next guest, Fareed Zakaria,
he is a well-known host

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.780
on CNN's GPS, a Washington
Post columnist, author,

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:19.780
and I would like to say, good
friend and mentor of myself.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.523
And I think, first of all,
good to see you, Fareed.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.270
If you can hear me.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:31.590
- I can hear you, Sal.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.440
It's a little bit jerky,
but I think we're all

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.940
getting used to life with
Zoom, which, I have to confess,

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:41.749
doesn't work as well as
the Khan Academy website.

00:01:41.749 --> 00:01:43.440
(Sal laughs)

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.991
- I don't know if that's a
veiled compliment or not.

00:01:45.991 --> 00:01:46.824
(Sal laughs)

00:01:46.824 --> 00:01:48.700
- That is a compliment,
that is a compliment.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:49.533
(Sal laughs)

00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:50.730
- Well, thanks for joining,

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:54.810
and you know, when we
schedule these things,

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.730
there was already a lot
going on in the world,

00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:01.163
and obviously, things are
just layering on top of it.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.250
I would love to just get your raw take

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.220
of, one, how are things
going for you personally,

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.290
but what's your sense of
what the world is like?

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:11.513
You're in New York.

00:02:13.436 --> 00:02:15.290
What do you think the
COVID layer is about?

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:16.900
What do you think the
economic layer is about?

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:21.900
And now what do you think
the kind of police brutality,

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:26.253
racial, systemic racism
layer is all about right now?

00:02:27.279 --> 00:02:28.729
(Fareed laughs)
- Oh my God.

00:02:30.271 --> 00:02:31.104
- Go.
(Sal laughs)

00:02:31.104 --> 00:02:35.683
- That's a big ask, but
so let me try and do it.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.050
It feels to me like at the very start,

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:40.170
it became clear to me,

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:44.460
and I wrote this the week
COVID kind of exploded,

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:48.680
that this was going to be a
series of cascading crises,

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.880
because we had never seen
anything quite like this,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.980
because you first have the pandemic.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:57.043
We realize, okay, we're in
a genuine global pandemic.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.650
And you don't have a lot
of actual global pandemics.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.170
SARS, MERS, these were regional.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.300
They never spread.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:09.330
So this is really the first
thing we have experienced,

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:13.260
where it really, I mean, I'm
sure you've noticed this, Sal,

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.383
when you call somebody
in India, Bangladesh,

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.270
you talk to somebody in Korea,

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:20.460
you talk to somebody in Europe,

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.496
they're all dealing with
exactly the same thing.

00:03:23.496 --> 00:03:25.260
There's a strange sense

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:29.563
in which we're united around this disease.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:33.570
Then you had the second
extraordinary thing,

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:36.090
which is historically
totally unprecedented,

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:39.400
which is we induced economic paralysis

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:44.025
in basically most of the
richest countries in the world.

00:03:44.025 --> 00:03:48.363
75% of the global economy
went into a lockdown.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.330
Nobody's ever done
anything like this before.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.900
That's why the numbers we were seeing,

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.730
frankly, in terms of the speed and scale,

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.610
dwarfed even the Great Depression,

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:01.400
because even with the Great
Depression, it took years to get

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.763
to the unemployment numbers
that we got to within weeks.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.210
So you've got that layer.

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:11.290
Then I think we are now going
through the third layer,

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:15.260
which is the widening
inequality of the response.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:18.130
So the rich world is
able to deal with this,

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.420
because it can print money.

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:22.583
Rich countries can borrow, essentially,

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:25.470
in an unlimited fashion.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:28.200
China, the United States, Germany.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.760
The United States above all

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.220
really has the ability to print money,

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:36.100
because it has kind of sovereign
capacity like nobody else.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:40.900
And so, while everyone's uneasy
about it, you can do a lot.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:44.330
I mean, the United
States did in three weeks

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:47.493
what it did in '08, '09, in three years,

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.620
and it is probably going to do more.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:53.920
But then you get to the part of the world

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:57.070
which can't do that, and you're
beginning to see that now,

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:59.180
with the India's, the Brazil's,

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:02.180
all these places where
the disease is spiraling,

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:04.380
they cannot lockdown any longer.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:06.720
A large part of the economy is day labor,

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.950
which means people make
the money that day,

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.810
spend the money that day on food, right?

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:13.840
So you're dealing with that,

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.830
and then you add to it the protests,

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:17.580
which I think are not unrelated.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.710
I mean, I think you've
got, in the United States,

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:24.710
40 million people
unemployed, nothing to do,

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:27.363
watching this brutality on television.

00:05:30.971 --> 00:05:31.960
What does all this do?

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:34.500
It produces nervousness, anxiety,

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.500
a sense of purposelessness, despair,

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:41.560
and then against that you
have, in America's case,

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.010
the oldest story in America,
the greatest tragedy

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:48.380
in American history,
playing out one more time.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:50.830
So I think that's the trajectory.

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:52.220
Now, personally, you asked.

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:56.240
I gotta confess, for me,
it hasn't been a big deal.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:57.800
I'm lucky.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:58.970
I have a large family.

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:00.148
So I've got three kids.

00:06:00.148 --> 00:06:01.970
I've gotten to see my kids

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:04.550
more than I've been
able to for a long time.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:05.810
My son is back from Yale.

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:08.250
My daughter's back from boarding school.

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:09.450
My little one is here.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:15.520
I've got a job which I
can do reasonably well,

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:19.600
where I can get about 90%
of technological capacity

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:22.300
and 110% of intellectual capacity.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:25.770
just because it's so
interesting and so engaging.

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:26.780
I've been working on a book.

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:28.663
That's easy to do from anywhere.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:34.343
I miss real life, but I've
been able to cope just fine.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:38.300
- And where do you see this going?

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:39.530
First of all, you did a great job

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:42.520
of being able to cover
all of those layers.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:44.630
But where do you see this going?

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:47.900
I mean, there's really two
layers that you're talking about

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:49.020
that I think are really interesting.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:51.300
One is that disparity between the rich

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.750
and the poor countries, and it seems like,

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.820
to your point, and I've
been studying this myself.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:57.880
It seems like the US does have

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:00.353
almost this infinite
capacity to print money.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:04.790
The federal government
can borrow the money

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:07.375
and distribute it, and it seems like,

00:07:07.375 --> 00:07:08.970
we could talk about what
the implications are,

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:10.030
other countries can't.

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:13.720
And then separately, you
have people who are angry,

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:16.880
rightfully angry about what
they are seeing in the world,

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:20.760
and it still has not changed,
and there is probably

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:23.653
an economic aspect that
it could morph into.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:27.640
I guess taking one at a time,
maybe the latter one first,

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:31.520
where do you see the protest evolving to?

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:33.107
Do you think it's going to be, you know,

00:07:33.107 --> 00:07:35.480
"Hey, this is how you have
to reform police training,"

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:37.280
and this and that? Do you
think it's gonna stay to that?

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:39.163
Or do you think it's going
to expand into other things?

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:41.700
- Here's the dilemma.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.660
I mean, this is, at some level,

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:46.680
the oldest story in American
history, which is the story

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:51.680
of the way in which blacks have
uniquely been treated badly

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:56.000
by the American system,
from the Constitution

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.970
into which they were written into slavery,

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:02.030
250 years working without
pay in the most brutal,

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:04.970
dehumanizing conditions,
their families broken up,

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:09.290
100 years of Jim Crow,
then mass incarceration.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:10.780
But there's another
piece of it, as you said,

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:12.430
which is truly about
the police and the way

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:16.710
the police handle things,
and they've come together.

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:17.770
We have a police force

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:20.210
that frankly looks more
like an army in most cities.

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:22.650
It behaves more like
an army in most cities.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:25.340
It's totally outside of
the black-white issue.

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:26.300
It's a problem.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:31.300
So, I think the problem for
the United States is this:

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:36.640
you can marshal the anger and the movement

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:40.700
for something like this at
a national level very well.

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:43.480
Martin Luther King did
it totally brilliantly.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:47.330
But he was asking, his
ask was a national ask,

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:50.210
the Civil Rights bill, fair housing.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:54.230
The ask here is that
18,000 police departments,

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:57.070
you heard that right, there
are 18,000 police departments

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:00.270
in America, have to reform themselves

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:04.260
and create different rules
that allow for bad police

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:06.710
to be penalized; frankly, to recruit

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:09.143
a different kind of
policeman in many cases.

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:11.180
How are you gonna do that?

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:13.550
I don't quite understand, you know?

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:16.090
And part of the frustration here, I think,

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:17.910
has been that people have seen,

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.060
look, we've been hearing good stuff

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:22.550
from the natural leaders for a while now.

00:09:22.550 --> 00:09:23.960
Every time something like this happens,

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:26.140
everybody agrees this is awful,

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:28.670
we need to do something
about it, but you have

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:33.020
that national unanimity
or agreement or consensus,

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:35.160
and somehow, it doesn't trickle down.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:37.810
It doesn't translate into a
difference in the day-to-day,

00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:42.000
lived experience of an
African-American, and this is why,

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:46.610
because most of that is
about local police chiefs,

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:50.940
local DAs, judges who are often elected.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:55.870
So we need a different model
where you take this passion,

00:09:55.870 --> 00:09:58.680
and instead of directing
it at Washington and Trump,

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:00.580
and God knows he deserves it,

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:02.900
but it's not gonna make
any difference, you know?

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:05.150
What you need to do is
at a very local level.

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:07.908
- And do you think that's
going to be effective?

00:10:07.908 --> 00:10:09.000
Do you think it's going to happen?

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:10.730
Obviously, it could be on
the police reform dimension.

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:11.660
It could be on the general

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:14.010
criminal justice reform dimension,

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:16.200
or it could be at the systemic racism,

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:18.940
which is a much broader notion, dimension.

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:23.070
Do you think this time is
different, or will the frustration

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:25.910
just continue, and three years from now,

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:28.890
we're back to where we
were, where people are angry

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:30.940
'cause something like this happens again?

00:10:31.836 --> 00:10:33.960
- I hope something happens,

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:36.060
but I think it's gonna be
hard in the short-term,

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:41.060
because to sustain this
kind of intensity of effort

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:44.340
at the local level requires
that people stay mobilized

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:46.630
at the local level, which
they have not tended to be.

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:50.270
So then you would need a push
from the federal government,

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:52.370
from, really, the president.

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:53.940
Clearly, Trump is not that president.

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:56.030
He's not gonna be the guy pushing this.

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:58.483
So maybe if you had a different president.

00:10:59.946 --> 00:11:03.930
You need some process,
because the crowds go away.

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:06.600
The police unions stay,
you know what I mean?

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:09.720
The forces that are
obstacles are very powerful,

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:11.580
local entrenched forces.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:15.790
The passion of the moment
cannot be maintained.

00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:17.675
This is what King was brilliant about.

00:11:17.675 --> 00:11:21.780
He figured out a way
to channel that passion

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:25.580
and to use it effectively
at strategic moments.

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:28.157
So what you need is something
like that at the local level,

00:11:28.157 --> 00:11:31.683
and the problem is you need
18,000 of those movements.

00:11:32.642 --> 00:11:34.160
- Yeah, yeah, and I would encourage

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:36.340
anyone watching on Facebook or YouTube,

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:40.150
please send in your comments.

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:41.773
Going into the economic layer,

00:11:41.773 --> 00:11:43.680
there's a question from Facebook.

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:47.137
Shoeb Mohammad asks, "What
will the future look like

00:11:47.137 --> 00:11:49.340
"in terms of from an
economic point of view?"

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:53.277
And then from Facebook, these
are related, Fred Edder asks,

00:11:53.277 --> 00:11:56.320
"Do you believe universal
basic income would help?"

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:57.820
What are your thoughts there?

00:11:57.820 --> 00:11:58.870
Will this be a minor?

00:11:58.870 --> 00:12:01.552
It looks like the stock market
thinks that nothing happened.

00:12:01.552 --> 00:12:04.283
(Sal laughs)

00:12:04.283 --> 00:12:06.429
How do you think it's gonna play out?

00:12:06.429 --> 00:12:08.570
- So, what the stock
market seems to suggest,

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:11.560
and look, the stock market
can also be right today

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:12.960
and then wrong tomorrow.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:14.733
We've all experienced that.

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:18.120
But if there's a message here, it is,

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:20.100
don't fight the most powerful central bank

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:21.500
in the history of the world.

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:24.850
You cannot fight the Fed.

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:28.350
The Fed has said that
they might buy bonds.

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:30.450
They might back junk bonds.

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:32.010
They might buy stocks.

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:34.097
The Fed is basically saying,

00:12:34.097 --> 00:12:38.167
"We have infinite capacity,
and we're willing to use it

00:12:38.167 --> 00:12:40.157
"on essentially infinite assets.

00:12:40.157 --> 00:12:42.007
"There is no asset that we regard

00:12:42.007 --> 00:12:44.270
"as being off-limits for us."

00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:48.260
In that circumstance, how
could an asset go down?

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:51.890
You have the floor

00:12:51.890 --> 00:12:53.927
of the most powerful central
bank in the world saying,

00:12:53.927 --> 00:12:56.120
"We're not gonna let this collapse."

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:59.650
Well, that's a wonderful
casino game to play,

00:12:59.650 --> 00:13:02.100
which is heads, I win,
tails, the Fed loses,

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:04.860
and the Fed can't lose, so you're fine.

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:09.060
So, I don't know how long can
the Fed continue to do that.

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:09.973
I mean, in a way,

00:13:11.677 --> 00:13:13.373
it's changing the rules
of capitalism, right?

00:13:13.373 --> 00:13:15.290
There's a sense in which you wonder,

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:17.200
and you have lectures on this,

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:19.780
what does price discovery mean anymore?

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:21.977
In a system where you
say, "Yeah, you know,

00:13:21.977 --> 00:13:24.467
"there are markets, the
prices rise and fall,

00:13:24.467 --> 00:13:27.960
"but if things get too
bad, the Fed will step in."

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:30.183
So, you have this kind of floor,

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:37.670
if the Fed says, "We'll
protect the downside."

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:39.720
So that's like, a meta-meta question.

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:42.350
Short-term, there's no
question, the United States

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:44.130
comes out of this the
best of all the countries

00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:47.150
in the world, because of the same reason

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:49.300
that it came out of the '08 crisis

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:50.563
the best of all the
countries in the world.

00:13:50.563 --> 00:13:51.983
I mean, people forget.

00:13:51.983 --> 00:13:55.540
We caused the financial crisis,
the global financial crisis.

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:57.460
It was the irresponsibility

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:59.770
of some combination of the private sector

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:02.930
and the public sector
in America that did it.

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:04.360
Who came out of it best?

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:06.070
Without any question, the United States.

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:07.620
Our banks are more dominant

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:09.813
in the world than they were before.

00:14:09.813 --> 00:14:11.610
It's the most extraordinary thing.

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:13.680
The banks, which were
supposed to be the origin

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:17.870
of this problem, are now
more powerful globally

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:18.930
than they've ever been before.

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:20.690
America's five biggest banks

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:22.220
have a larger market share than anyone.

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:26.650
So, there's a long-run question of,

00:14:26.650 --> 00:14:27.887
there's article in
Foreign Affairs that says,

00:14:27.887 --> 00:14:31.380
"Can you have magic money forever?"

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:33.300
I don't know the question to that.

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:34.590
Nobody knows the question to that.

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:39.590
I remember when Japan crossed
the 75% debt to GDP ratio.

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:41.500
People said, this is it.

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:43.943
No country has ever
done anything like this.

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:46.340
We're in uncharted waters.

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:49.080
This is gonna cause
huge problems for Japan.

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:53.360
Japan is currently at
250% debt to GDP ratio.

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:58.360
So maybe it's something a small
number of countries can do.

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:01.550
I think the most important
thing to remember is,

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:03.080
India can't borrow like that.

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:04.520
Turkey can't borrow like that.

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:05.830
Brazil can't borrow like that.

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:09.280
They borrow in short-term
debt, in dollars,

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:11.080
which means they have to pay it back,

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:13.720
and if their currency depreciates,
they have to pay more.

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:15.633
So that's very tough.

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:21.150
So I think for the US, the
big part that I don't think

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:23.510
has been fully factored
in by the market is,

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:26.310
this is going to be a very
slow, halting recovery.

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:29.060
I mean, you're not gonna have a crash

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:31.080
and a collapse because of the Fed.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:34.247
But the Fed can't force people to fly.

00:15:36.156 --> 00:15:38.610
The Fed can't force people
to go to restaurants.

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:41.350
It can't force them to go to
movie theaters that are empty.

00:15:41.350 --> 00:15:44.730
It can't force the NFL
to start holding games.

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:48.060
So all that spending is
not going to take place.

00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:51.717
Now, the simplest
principle of economics is,

00:15:51.717 --> 00:15:53.810
"My spending is your income."

00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:58.810
So if spending is down,
how can the economy

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:03.290
be anything but at a much lower capacity?

00:16:03.290 --> 00:16:05.530
China is finding that
the industrial production

00:16:05.530 --> 00:16:08.980
is up to 90% of pre-crisis,

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:11.530
but consumption is not even up to 50%.

00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:12.720
So the Chinese are now trying

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:14.390
to give free gift certificates,

00:16:14.390 --> 00:16:17.650
I think two billion dollars of literally,

00:16:17.650 --> 00:16:21.730
you know, imagine getting
coupons on the street,

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:24.050
which you can just spend at retail

00:16:24.050 --> 00:16:26.380
as a way of inducing expenditure.

00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:28.810
I wonder whether that's what
we will have to deal with.

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:31.610
'Cause we're not at the
universal basic income level.

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:34.000
That's kind of solving
a different problem,

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:37.670
which is technology and
automation, globalization,

00:16:37.670 --> 00:16:39.780
making it so large numbers of people

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:42.093
don't have enough to do and
can't make enough money.

00:16:42.093 --> 00:16:44.570
What we have right now
as a problem is we just

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:48.410
can't get people to spend
money, because what do they do?

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:49.590
They can't take a vacation.

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:51.460
They can't get on an airplane.

00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:52.810
So how do you force that?

00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:55.363
How do you force
expenditure into the system?

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:57.596
- Yeah, that's super helpful.

00:16:57.596 --> 00:17:00.020
For those watching, I remember growing up,

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:00.930
I was always confused.

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:02.880
When people talk about
central banks print money,

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:04.200
where does the money go?

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:07.451
To just double click on that,
what Fareed's describing is,

00:17:07.451 --> 00:17:09.630
especially the Federal
Reserve of the United States,

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:10.880
which is quasi-independent,

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:12.550
but really a governmental institution,

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:13.860
can essentially print money.

00:17:13.860 --> 00:17:14.800
Where does it go?

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:16.390
It goes and buys assets.

00:17:16.390 --> 00:17:18.510
Normally, it buys government debt,

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:20.800
so it's essentially lending
money to the federal government.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:23.500
The more it does that, the
cost of the debt goes down.

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:26.149
But now it's buying almost everything,

00:17:26.149 --> 00:17:28.980
(Sal laughs)
is what Fareed's saying.

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:30.930
So you can imagine, as the
cost of debt goes down,

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:32.110
then the federal government

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:33.940
can borrow more and more and more

00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:36.520
and do these trillion
dollar at-a-time stimulus,

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:38.140
and at the same time, the Federal Reserve

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:41.510
can get more aggressive and
buy debt from companies,

00:17:41.510 --> 00:17:43.210
maybe even one day think
about buying things

00:17:43.210 --> 00:17:46.440
like equities and be
a floor to the market.

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:48.680
But you know, there's an
interesting question there.

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:50.370
Your point, maybe the Fed could engineer

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:53.606
so at least the market has a floor to it,

00:17:53.606 --> 00:17:56.783
and certain parts of the
economy, the debt markets,

00:17:57.680 --> 00:17:59.890
but that benefits, and I think we saw this

00:17:59.890 --> 00:18:02.440
coming out of the financial
crisis, it benefits homeowners.

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:05.080
You can refinance, and
your mortgage goes down.

00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:07.650
It benefits people who
have 401(k)'s and stock

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:12.650
and shareholders, but the
people I've just described

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:15.160
are the more well off already.

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:19.550
What happens to, I don't know
if it's universal basic income

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:21.840
or this gift certificate-type program.

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:25.690
What happens to the people who
are waiters and waitresses,

00:18:25.690 --> 00:18:27.960
small restaurant owners,

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:30.453
Airbnbing their house on a small scale?

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:35.170
How do they live is not
an obvious question,

00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:36.517
and not an obvious answer.

00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:42.990
- Yeah, so that's the
broader, the bigger question.

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:45.550
Just one factoid which
I think would be helpful

00:18:45.550 --> 00:18:48.630
for you to understand,
so everything you said,

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:50.660
of course, about the Fed is exactly right.

00:18:50.660 --> 00:18:53.120
Now, keep in mind, because
of all of the factors

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:54.560
you are describing, the federal government

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:58.200
is currently borrowing at .8%.

00:18:58.200 --> 00:18:59.960
So think about all you homeowners

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:03.853
who managed to refinance
heroically at 3.25 or 3.5.

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:08.970
The government is borrowing
at 0.8%, 10-year bonds.

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:12.430
So, at some level, it's very
sensible for it to be borrowing

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:15.440
the money, as long as it
spends it on useful things.

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:16.840
That's the part which I've always felt.

00:19:16.840 --> 00:19:18.710
The government has borrowed a lot of money

00:19:18.710 --> 00:19:20.470
in the past 10, 15 years

00:19:20.470 --> 00:19:22.440
and spent it on tax cuts for the rich,

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:25.220
which is the most idiotic idea,

00:19:25.220 --> 00:19:28.150
because it is not
fundamentally productive.

00:19:28.150 --> 00:19:30.170
But what you're describing is the problem

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:33.730
that we already have, which
was this rising inequality.

00:19:33.730 --> 00:19:37.320
And COVID is actually exacerbating it.

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:39.340
It's exacerbating it on three levels.

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:40.887
The gap between the richest countries

00:19:40.887 --> 00:19:41.900
in the world and the poorest

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:44.350
is going to explain for
the reasons we described.

00:19:45.220 --> 00:19:47.600
The gap between the biggest
companies of the world

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:50.160
and the small mom and
pop's is gonna expand,

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:53.430
because in this digital
world, what's happened

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:57.600
is we've had a flight to
digital, a flight to size,

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:01.940
a flight to security, all of
which mean Amazon gets bigger,

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:04.040
and the small mom and pop get smaller.

00:20:04.040 --> 00:20:05.480
Google gets bigger.

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:09.522
All these big platforms
get bigger and bigger,

00:20:09.522 --> 00:20:13.040
and even in the non-tech world,

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:14.620
you're gonna see that same thing happen.

00:20:14.620 --> 00:20:15.453
The biggest companies

00:20:15.453 --> 00:20:18.650
will be able to borrow
at much cheaper rates.

00:20:18.650 --> 00:20:21.230
Then finally, for people.

00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:24.080
The COVID divide is
kind of a class divide.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:26.190
Who can work from home?

00:20:26.190 --> 00:20:30.530
It's digital workers,
service workers, consultants,

00:20:30.530 --> 00:20:31.510
people like you and me.

00:20:31.510 --> 00:20:34.100
Who anything work from home?

00:20:34.100 --> 00:20:36.860
It's people who work with their hands,

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:40.200
people who generally
have lower-paying jobs,

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:42.870
lower status in many ways, unfortunately.

00:20:42.870 --> 00:20:44.270
I'm not saying it's the right thing,

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:48.230
but the reality is that
that divide, again, expands.

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:51.060
So how do you solve it?

00:20:51.060 --> 00:20:54.060
Look, I tend to think
that a better solution

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:58.050
than universal basic income
is a massive expansion

00:20:58.050 --> 00:21:00.430
of the many programs we have

00:21:00.430 --> 00:21:03.070
that would achieve something very similar.

00:21:03.070 --> 00:21:05.270
My favorite program of
the federal government

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:07.810
is the most difficult to explain,

00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:11.340
and my theory is that's
why it doesn't do well,

00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:12.810
because nobody understands it.

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:14.930
It's called the Earned Income Tax Credit,

00:21:14.930 --> 00:21:17.490
and basically what it is
is the government says,

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:20.140
if you work, we are going to make sure

00:21:20.140 --> 00:21:23.170
that you're never below the poverty line.

00:21:23.170 --> 00:21:25.077
We're gonna pick an
arbitrary Number and say,

00:21:25.077 --> 00:21:26.387
"You work?

00:21:26.387 --> 00:21:28.737
"For whatever reason, the
private sector does not pay you

00:21:28.737 --> 00:21:32.030
"enough to cross this
line, we will top it off."

00:21:32.030 --> 00:21:34.410
The great thing about that
is that it incentivizes work,

00:21:34.410 --> 00:21:35.810
it keeps the private sector alive,

00:21:35.810 --> 00:21:38.060
it doesn't distort prices

00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:40.870
the way that minimum
wages do have that effect.

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:42.010
You create a minimum wage,

00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:44.220
and there is some job destruction,

00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:47.070
because there are low-level entry jobs

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:48.440
that won't be available.

00:21:48.440 --> 00:21:50.390
This does none of that.

00:21:50.390 --> 00:21:51.570
It's more effective.

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:53.280
It's a program that, frankly,

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:56.330
Milton Friedman and
Bernie Sanders both like,

00:21:56.330 --> 00:21:58.354
and yet, it's hard to expand.

00:21:58.354 --> 00:21:59.920
It's sort of complicated.

00:21:59.920 --> 00:22:02.210
It's easier to explain minimum wage.

00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:05.230
So if you could take a program like that

00:22:05.230 --> 00:22:06.969
and double or triple it in size,

00:22:06.969 --> 00:22:09.910
if you could take food stamps,

00:22:09.910 --> 00:22:13.630
if you could take Medicaid
and things like that,

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:16.300
I mean, I think the key
should be to make it

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:20.172
so that Americans are
confronting a changing world

00:22:20.172 --> 00:22:23.510
with many, many different
forces buffeting them.

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:25.770
But they don't have to go into it naked.

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:27.981
They don't have to go into it unarmed.

00:22:27.981 --> 00:22:30.600
They can have the security of healthcare.

00:22:30.600 --> 00:22:32.990
They can have the security
of good nutritional programs.

00:22:32.990 --> 00:22:35.520
They can have the security of retraining.

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:37.180
If you give them all that,

00:22:37.180 --> 00:22:39.077
then they can handle a world like this.

00:22:39.077 --> 00:22:40.580
But if you don't give them that,

00:22:40.580 --> 00:22:42.840
then I think you make it very difficult.

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:46.870
And the problems with universal
basic income are obvious.

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:49.865
There's a question of whether
you de-incentivize work,

00:22:49.865 --> 00:22:51.833
you destroy the market a lot.

00:22:53.320 --> 00:22:55.550
A lot of what it does
I think doesn't address

00:22:55.550 --> 00:22:57.820
one of the core problems
that we're gonna face

00:22:57.820 --> 00:22:58.910
in the world going forward,

00:22:58.910 --> 00:23:01.960
which is it's not just
that people need money.

00:23:01.960 --> 00:23:04.320
They need something to do.

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:05.610
They need a calling.

00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:07.420
They need the dignity of work.

00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:12.020
They need the sense of
status that that gives you.

00:23:12.020 --> 00:23:15.650
I think honestly, particularly
for men, a lot of men

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:20.110
derive their sense of worth
from things like that,

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:22.096
and women are more complex, I think,

00:23:22.096 --> 00:23:25.760
because they've always had the reality

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:30.260
of being mothers and caretakers.

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:32.040
They frankly have a more sophisticated

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:35.540
understanding of internal worth,

00:23:35.540 --> 00:23:37.480
which is a little more socially grounded.

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:38.850
Maybe it's a construct.

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:40.340
Maybe we should move away from it.

00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:42.630
But the truth is that's where we are now.

00:23:42.630 --> 00:23:46.190
And I think that you put two
or three million truck drivers

00:23:46.190 --> 00:23:48.910
out of work because we've
got self-driving cars,

00:23:48.910 --> 00:23:51.550
and just saying to them,
"Here's a handout,"

00:23:51.550 --> 00:23:54.350
I don't think you've solved the problem.

00:23:54.350 --> 00:23:55.270
- Yeah, that's a good point,

00:23:55.270 --> 00:23:59.330
and I like the Earned Income Tax Credit.

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:02.140
I mean, I guess one way to
view it is just negative taxes.

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:05.440
Right now, you imagine the
first X dollars is not taxed,

00:24:05.440 --> 00:24:06.620
and every increment above that,

00:24:06.620 --> 00:24:09.800
from, say, 20 to 30,000 is taxed at 10%.

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:12.373
That first bracket is
essentially negative.

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:16.910
- At some level, the
government gives you money.

00:24:16.910 --> 00:24:18.330
At some crossover level,

00:24:18.330 --> 00:24:20.220
you start giving the government money.

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:21.460
- Exactly, exactly.

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:23.510
No, but I agree, it makes a lot of sense.

00:24:25.090 --> 00:24:27.543
So, I mean, a lot of questions here.

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:32.500
Actually, I mean, you know, one of 'em,

00:24:32.500 --> 00:24:35.657
Dan Companion on Facebook,
maybe we'll end with this,

00:24:35.657 --> 00:24:38.365
"Is money just an abstract mechanism?"

00:24:38.365 --> 00:24:41.470
I guess I'll change that into
a slightly different question.

00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:42.820
He's asking about financial form,

00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:43.653
what we should do post-COVID.

00:24:43.653 --> 00:24:45.555
- Yes, the answer to
that of course is yes.

00:24:45.555 --> 00:24:46.388
(Sal laughs)

00:24:46.388 --> 00:24:48.400
- The answer on the money is yes.

00:24:48.400 --> 00:24:49.250
But I guess one question,

00:24:49.250 --> 00:24:51.960
we talked a lot about with
the Fed can do in the US.

00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:53.800
What's the negative?

00:24:53.800 --> 00:24:56.090
What's the potential limiter on it?

00:24:56.090 --> 00:24:58.150
Inflation, is it something else?

00:24:58.150 --> 00:25:02.070
And then just to finish
up, how do you think

00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:04.250
the world's gonna be
in two or three years,

00:25:04.250 --> 00:25:05.900
or how do you think it should be?

00:25:07.930 --> 00:25:12.030
- Yeah, so the limiting
effect for a central bank

00:25:12.030 --> 00:25:14.250
is usually, it takes two forms.

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:18.490
One is your currency
collapses, and second,

00:25:18.490 --> 00:25:20.670
you've got the hyperinflation,
and the two are linked.

00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:22.170
And the basic idea is simple,

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:25.310
which is if people think
you're managing your affairs

00:25:25.310 --> 00:25:26.630
really irresponsibly,

00:25:26.630 --> 00:25:29.620
if you have debts that
you clearly can't pay,

00:25:29.620 --> 00:25:32.183
basically, people start
fleeing your currency.

00:25:33.100 --> 00:25:34.950
Nobody wants to buy your government bonds

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:36.410
and things like that.

00:25:36.410 --> 00:25:39.593
I think the US is far, far
away from that scenario.

00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:43.770
The end of the day, the taxing potential

00:25:43.770 --> 00:25:47.340
of the United States
government is enormous.

00:25:47.340 --> 00:25:49.600
I mean, just to give one simple example,

00:25:49.600 --> 00:25:50.680
the United States on average

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:53.860
has eight percent sales taxes, right?

00:25:53.860 --> 00:25:57.280
Europe on average has 25% sales taxes.

00:25:57.280 --> 00:25:58.480
So I'm not saying it's going to happen,

00:25:58.480 --> 00:26:00.910
but what I'm saying is in extremis,

00:26:00.910 --> 00:26:02.210
if the United States government

00:26:02.210 --> 00:26:04.640
had to find a way to make money,

00:26:04.640 --> 00:26:09.470
it's very easy to imagine
doubling the sales tax.

00:26:09.470 --> 00:26:12.160
It's very easy to imagine moderate rises

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:13.824
in income tax for the middle class.

00:26:13.824 --> 00:26:16.623
If you're talking about
huge amounts of money,

00:26:16.623 --> 00:26:19.660
obviously for the wealthy,
you can do a wealth tax.

00:26:19.660 --> 00:26:21.820
Again, I'm not saying
these things are all good.

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:23.940
But the United States
is not in a situation

00:26:23.940 --> 00:26:25.457
where you would say to
yourself, "Oh my God,

00:26:25.457 --> 00:26:27.137
"they've got these big budgets,

00:26:27.137 --> 00:26:30.150
"and there's no way they
could ever pay this back."

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:31.410
That's just not where we are.

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:35.330
So I think, you know,
my feeling is the focus

00:26:35.330 --> 00:26:37.960
should more be on what
are you spending it on.

00:26:37.960 --> 00:26:40.500
If you're taking another
trillion dollars out,

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:43.270
it would be great to spend
it on infrastructure.

00:26:43.270 --> 00:26:45.530
Frankly, that's all the
people you'd employ,

00:26:45.530 --> 00:26:48.020
are exactly the kind of people
we've been talking about

00:26:48.020 --> 00:26:51.080
who are having challenges,
people who work with their hands,

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:52.560
people without college degrees,

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:54.660
people who maybe don't work in big cities.

00:26:55.736 --> 00:26:58.220
There's a huge double whammy here,

00:26:58.220 --> 00:27:01.630
which is you help the
economy by creating greater

00:27:01.630 --> 00:27:03.840
and more efficient
transportation and communication,

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:06.040
but you also help these people have

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:08.340
really interesting jobs
with dignity and such.

00:27:10.739 --> 00:27:11.572
(Fareed laughs)

00:27:11.572 --> 00:27:12.880
The small question of where we'll be

00:27:12.880 --> 00:27:14.280
two or three years from now,

00:27:15.770 --> 00:27:20.540
I think that, you know,
there's a part of me

00:27:20.540 --> 00:27:24.620
that feels like it's easy to exaggerate

00:27:24.620 --> 00:27:27.550
the degree to which there will be change.

00:27:27.550 --> 00:27:31.180
After the influenza of 1918, after all,

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:34.010
don't forget, the next big
thing that happened in America

00:27:34.010 --> 00:27:36.830
was called the Jazz Age, the Roaring 20s.

00:27:36.830 --> 00:27:40.530
So people got back to restaurants.

00:27:40.530 --> 00:27:42.530
In fact, they went back so fast

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:45.900
that even though you had
Prohibition in place,

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:48.843
alcohol was illegal, you
still had the Jazz Age,

00:27:48.843 --> 00:27:50.130
and you still had the Roaring 20s.

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:53.560
So people obviously, you could even argue

00:27:53.560 --> 00:27:54.930
they were desperate to congregate.

00:27:54.930 --> 00:27:56.060
They were desperate to gather.

00:27:56.060 --> 00:27:59.699
So I never bought the idea
that what you're gonna see

00:27:59.699 --> 00:28:03.500
is a kind of replacement
of life with digital life.

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:05.640
I think you see some level of it,

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:08.971
but you're also going to see
enormous amount of social.

00:28:08.971 --> 00:28:12.280
Aristotle said man is a
social and political animal,

00:28:12.280 --> 00:28:14.050
and I think that's very true.

00:28:14.050 --> 00:28:16.820
But I do think you're going
to see the acceleration

00:28:16.820 --> 00:28:19.563
of a lot of these trends
that are already in place,

00:28:21.227 --> 00:28:22.810
for the digitization of life.

00:28:22.810 --> 00:28:24.863
I think that every big company,

00:28:26.298 --> 00:28:28.630
WeWork may have failed, but every company

00:28:28.630 --> 00:28:30.850
will become WeWork, by which I mean,

00:28:30.850 --> 00:28:32.047
every company is going to say,

00:28:32.047 --> 00:28:36.870
"Why do you need to come in
to the office nine to five?"

00:28:36.870 --> 00:28:40.880
I remember in your book, you
talk about how the school

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:45.870
is an outgrowth of the
19th century factory model,

00:28:45.870 --> 00:28:47.740
where everybody gets in one place,

00:28:47.740 --> 00:28:49.560
you throw information at them,

00:28:49.560 --> 00:28:51.200
they get through the conveyor belt.

00:28:51.200 --> 00:28:52.740
By the time they're 18, they're out,

00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:55.550
when with a modern
office it's very similar.

00:28:55.550 --> 00:28:56.830
Why does everybody need

00:28:56.830 --> 00:28:59.120
to be in the same place at the same time?

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:01.333
Why do they need to work the same hours?

00:29:02.480 --> 00:29:04.460
Much more efficient
probably would be a system

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:05.873
where people come and go as they want.

00:29:05.873 --> 00:29:07.320
There'd still be an office.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:09.070
I think there's still enormous importance

00:29:09.070 --> 00:29:10.300
of real-life meeting.

00:29:10.300 --> 00:29:11.990
But some people come in three days a week,

00:29:11.990 --> 00:29:14.010
some people come in two,
some people come in five.

00:29:14.010 --> 00:29:16.270
It varies week to week.

00:29:16.270 --> 00:29:20.653
Maybe you don't need a personal
space that is your desk.

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:24.140
That's a very inefficient
way to us assets,

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:25.340
if you think about it.

00:29:25.340 --> 00:29:27.590
Why not have the WeWork formula,

00:29:27.590 --> 00:29:30.700
where these are all shared,
they are used by everyone?

00:29:30.700 --> 00:29:33.080
Maybe everyone has a
personal closet or locker

00:29:33.080 --> 00:29:35.460
or something like that where
you keep some of your stuff.

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:36.293
When you're there, you use it.

00:29:36.293 --> 00:29:38.633
Everything is up in the cloud anyway.

00:29:39.755 --> 00:29:44.200
And if you do that, and you
reduce office demand by 25%,

00:29:44.200 --> 00:29:47.100
that's a huge impact on
the way urban life changes.

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:49.240
So I think that's just one example.

00:29:49.240 --> 00:29:50.920
I think those are the kind of changes

00:29:50.920 --> 00:29:51.753
you're going to see

00:29:51.753 --> 00:29:55.260
that are the most
important from daily life.

00:29:55.260 --> 00:29:57.370
The biggest change that's gonna happen

00:29:57.370 --> 00:29:59.980
that impacts the kind
of world we live in is,

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:03.820
are you going to see an
acceleration of nationalism,

00:30:03.820 --> 00:30:08.320
and in particular, an acceleration
of the US-China conflict?

00:30:08.320 --> 00:30:10.370
Because if you look forward
and say to yourself,

00:30:10.370 --> 00:30:12.090
there are two possible models,

00:30:12.090 --> 00:30:14.910
one where this pandemic,
which is global in nature,

00:30:14.910 --> 00:30:17.657
brings us together to realize, you know,

00:30:17.657 --> 00:30:19.620
"We kind of need a better WHO."

00:30:21.110 --> 00:30:23.850
By that, we mean we need
one that's better resourced,

00:30:23.850 --> 00:30:26.260
better staffed, and actually has the power

00:30:26.260 --> 00:30:30.340
to tell countries, to push
back against countries,

00:30:30.340 --> 00:30:32.118
which the WHO does not have,

00:30:32.118 --> 00:30:33.660
whether it's against China or the US,

00:30:33.660 --> 00:30:35.950
and those rules were written by the US,

00:30:35.950 --> 00:30:38.217
because we don't want the WHO telling us,

00:30:38.217 --> 00:30:40.840
"You're not doing a good
job on this or that."

00:30:40.840 --> 00:30:43.780
Or do we go down the path of Cold War II,

00:30:43.780 --> 00:30:47.690
where this whole thing becomes
really fundamentally about.

00:30:47.690 --> 00:30:48.838
- Cold War II, I like that.

00:30:48.838 --> 00:30:50.490
- And dominated by a geopolitical, right,

00:30:50.490 --> 00:30:54.120
geopolitical, geo-economic,
geo-technological struggle

00:30:54.120 --> 00:30:56.240
between the United
States/China, which will be

00:30:56.240 --> 00:30:58.183
completely new, because it is

00:30:58.183 --> 00:31:00.533
between the two biggest
economies in the world.

00:31:01.450 --> 00:31:04.980
US in 1950 was 50% of global GDP.

00:31:04.980 --> 00:31:07.620
The Soviet Union was 15.
- Wow.

00:31:07.620 --> 00:31:10.750
- China and the US will
be pure competitors,

00:31:10.750 --> 00:31:12.530
and that is a whole new world

00:31:12.530 --> 00:31:15.050
and a very dangerous
world and a divided world.

00:31:15.050 --> 00:31:17.640
So which one of those we live in,

00:31:17.640 --> 00:31:19.313
I think, is the big question.

00:31:20.290 --> 00:31:21.450
- Wow.

00:31:21.450 --> 00:31:23.260
Well, Fareed, you've given
us a lot to talk about,

00:31:23.260 --> 00:31:24.110
and hopefully you've enjoyed.

00:31:24.110 --> 00:31:28.700
In your day job, I know you get
to write on a lot of layers,

00:31:28.700 --> 00:31:31.400
but on TV, you're always
asking folks questions.

00:31:31.400 --> 00:31:34.139
Even when I see you, I know
that there's a lot in your head

00:31:34.139 --> 00:31:37.220
(Sal laughs)
that needs to come out.

00:31:37.220 --> 00:31:39.420
I think for anyone who's watching this,

00:31:39.420 --> 00:31:42.460
I think, has a new
appreciation for Fareed's depth

00:31:42.460 --> 00:31:44.530
and breadth of knowledge in all things.

00:31:44.530 --> 00:31:47.570
So, I learn something every time we chat.

00:31:47.570 --> 00:31:49.820
So thank you so much,
Fareed, for joining us.

00:31:51.357 --> 00:31:52.580
- Oh, it's my pleasure.

00:31:52.580 --> 00:31:55.000
I'm a huge fan of Khan Academy.

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:58.120
I would only reiterate the point you made.

00:31:58.120 --> 00:32:00.600
What you've done is really revolutionary.

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:03.870
It is a non-profit, and
anyone who's listening in

00:32:03.870 --> 00:32:07.100
should realize that if you
think about bang for your buck,

00:32:07.100 --> 00:32:09.300
if you think about how much of an impact

00:32:09.300 --> 00:32:11.010
you can have on helping people,

00:32:11.010 --> 00:32:13.810
if you believe that
education makes a difference,

00:32:13.810 --> 00:32:15.940
this is the best way to scale it.

00:32:15.940 --> 00:32:20.283
So when you're done with
this, donate some money.

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:23.020
I tell you what, I'll do the same,

00:32:23.020 --> 00:32:25.129
so you guys have to follow me.

00:32:25.129 --> 00:32:26.180
- No, Fareed's been very kind.

00:32:26.180 --> 00:32:27.410
He's helped us over the years,

00:32:27.410 --> 00:32:29.530
and Fareed, I definitely
view you as family.

00:32:29.530 --> 00:32:31.310
I tell everyone, for those
who are not watching,

00:32:31.310 --> 00:32:33.100
we once had to, our families,

00:32:33.100 --> 00:32:34.300
we went to the same conference,

00:32:34.300 --> 00:32:35.930
and both of us dragged
our whole families along.

00:32:35.930 --> 00:32:38.780
So they put us in the same house together.

00:32:38.780 --> 00:32:42.540
I woke up one morning, and
you were feeding Imran cereal.

00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:44.670
So anyone who's fed my son cereal

00:32:44.670 --> 00:32:46.860
in the morning I consider family.

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:48.423
So thank you so much, Fareed.

00:32:52.180 --> 00:32:53.843
- Great pleasure, all best.

00:32:55.610 --> 00:32:57.180
- Well, thanks, everyone, for joining.

00:32:57.180 --> 00:32:58.560
Hopefully you enjoyed that conversation.

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:00.800
As I said, every time I talk to Fareed,

00:33:00.800 --> 00:33:02.740
you can kind of talk about
anything and everything,

00:33:02.740 --> 00:33:05.020
and he's got unusual depth and insight.

00:33:05.020 --> 00:33:06.390
So hopefully you enjoyed that,

00:33:06.390 --> 00:33:08.250
and I look forward to
seeing everyone tomorrow.

00:33:08.250 --> 00:33:10.540
We're gonna have actually a
Khan Academy board member,

00:33:10.540 --> 00:33:12.490
James Manyika, who's also the head

00:33:12.490 --> 00:33:14.220
of the McKinsey Global Institute.

00:33:14.220 --> 00:33:18.179
He's done a lot of work on the
future of work and learning,

00:33:18.179 --> 00:33:20.560
obviously one of our board members,

00:33:20.560 --> 00:33:21.950
and obviously we'll talk a lot

00:33:21.950 --> 00:33:23.570
about what's going on
in the world generally.

00:33:23.570 --> 00:33:25.560
So look forward to seeing all of y'all,

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:28.613
noon Pacific, three p.m. Eastern tomorrow.

